{"summary": "On 2026-11-18 a fault was injected on purpose into a payments system on one machine. The system ran with synthetic data and a simulated provider, so no real money moved. Deploy 133 set LEDGER_HANDLER=naive on the ledger consumer, so the consumer booked resent provider webhooks a second time. The alert MoneyCheckFailed fired 17.7 s after the fault. Klaxon diagnosed the cause, and the grading rule found that it named it. Klaxon then asked at the approval gate to redeploy the ledger consumer with LEDGER_HANDLER=idempotent. The request was approved and ran as deploy 134. Code verified the action and it passed. The cause was removed and the system was healthy. The money was still not right. At the last check, 36 events were counted more than once and our books were $1,518.19 ahead of the provider's balance. That damage stays until a person repairs it.", "timeline": "At 05:52:13 UTC the alert rules were quiet at 2 evaluations in a row, and the money check passed. The fault's injection then began, and it took 2.7 s. The deploy log records it as deploy 133 at 05:52:16 UTC. At 05:52:31 UTC MoneyCheckFailed fired. At 05:52:32 UTC Klaxon's loop began to investigate. At 05:54:08 UTC the loop stopped as diagnosed, after 11 steps and 7 tool calls, in 95.8 s. At 05:54:09 UTC Klaxon made request r-7dee at the gate. The request was approved at 05:56:02 UTC, after it had been open 113 s. At 05:56:05 UTC deploy 134 ran. Health checks from 05:56:06 UTC to 05:56:22 UTC found the stack healthy. A later money check at 05:57:05 UTC still failed. Verification passed at 292 s after the fault. The harness's clearing at 292.2 s found the good settings already running and changed nothing. At 05:58:36 UTC the rules read the stack again, and MoneyCheckFailed fired again.", "impact": "The synthetic clients saw no failures. 1327 payments arrived and 1327 succeeded. 0 were refused, and 0 were left with their outcome unknown. 127 refunds succeeded. The clients retried 44 times. The books were wrong, and the damage grew. At the alert the money check failed 2 of its 5 invariants, with posted_exactly_once at 7 and matches_provider at 8. Our ledger was then $493.23 ahead of the provider's balance. Right before the request there were 41 findings: 20 events were counted more than once, and our books were $1,097.74 ahead. Right after the action there were 73 findings: 36 events were counted more than once, and our books were $1,518.19 ahead. So the damage grew while the request waited for approval. The later check at 05:57:05 UTC found the same 36 events and the same $1,518.19. After the clearing, posted_exactly_once stood at 36 and matches_provider at 37, with the same $1,518.19. The evidence does not explain why matches_provider rose to 37.", "cause": "The recorded cause was a deploy of the ledger consumer without its dedupe, with LEDGER_HANDLER=naive. When the provider sent a webhook again after an acknowledgement it never got, the consumer booked it a second time. These double bookings broke the posted_exactly_once and matches_provider invariants, and that failure raised MoneyCheckFailed. Klaxon's diagnosis had a confidence of 0.85. It said the ledger consumer had been redeployed with the naive handler, that it no longer deduplicated provider events, and that the provider resends webhooks. A keyword rule, rubric version 4, graded the diagnosis as naming the cause. It found all 3 required elements and the deploy that brought the fault. It also found that the proposed fix fits the remedy. The rule reads words, not meaning. The diagnosis's own numbers and times were not checked by code. One of its times differs from the deploy log's.", "response": "Klaxon had read-only tools and one write action, rollback_deploy, which runs only if a person approves it. It asked at the gate in request r-7dee to redeploy the ledger consumer from LEDGER_HANDLER naive to idempotent, with LEDGER_BAD_EVENTS left at dead_letter. The request said Klaxon would not undo the change by going back to the naive handler, though a person could do that by hand. The diagnosis's own rollback plan did suggest redeploying the naive settings if the consumer misbehaved, and that conflicts with the request. The request was approved with make approve, typed at a terminal. The gate's log records how the decision was made, not who made it. Deploy 134 ran at 05:56:05 UTC, 214 s after the alert. Code then verified the action over 61.2 s. All containers were healthy and the rules were quiet at 2 evaluations in a row. Nothing the provider made after the action was booked wrong. The money was still not right.", "repairs": "The books still need repair. At the last check, at 05:57:05 UTC, 36 events were counted more than once, 36 payments had books that differ from the provider's, and our books were $1,518.19 ahead of the provider's balance. The examples included evt_000308 posted 3 times and evt_000251 posted 2 times. A rollback repairs nothing already booked. The recorded remedy is to reverse each double posting. That is a write, so it needs approval. Repairs that move money are not among Klaxon's actions, so a person must make them and then rerun the money check. The evidence also leaves open why the check after the clearing showed matches_provider at 37, and that should be looked at during the repair."}
```

